! mcp_bootstrap_p168.strap -- THE WAIT ROUTINE, the whole of it:
! K WAIT, K DIG, K MISU, K E LOOP and K PASS, printed page 168.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.175, header READ and not
! assumed: the top line prints "TIME CLOCK 00054041  PUNID
! 11D11MCP   CARD ID JA000001   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE
! NUMBER  168".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD IS BLANK. <<<
! Rendered at 400 dpi across the full 6800-pixel width of the
! form: the word "LOCATION" is printed at the right-hand end of
! the heading row and NO DIGITS FOLLOW IT.  That is exactly what
! docs/transcription_workflow.md predicts -- the field is LINE
! 1'S location, and line 1 here is an asterisk banner that
! compiles nothing.  A blank is NOT a zero; nothing was read off
! the dashed rule.  This page therefore has no independent
! witness to its origin word except its own printed "SLC,$".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-SEVEN PRINTED LINES.  Three asterisk-banner comment
! cards, one "SLC,$", and thirty-three instructions -- nineteen
! half words and fourteen full words -- and then blank paper to
! the foot of the form.  No DD, no SYN, no VF, no reservation, no
! forward SLC: the page is CONTIGUOUS from 220347.40 to
! 220377.00 with no hole of any kind.
!
! Five entry points, and the IBM manual draws all five:
!
!   lines  1-3   the banner, "********** WAIT **********".
!
!   line   4     "SLC,$".  Its printed LOCATION, 220347.40, is
!                the counter ARRIVING from printed p.167.
!
!   lines  5-15  K WAIT.  C22-6728-1 printed p.205 ("Wait
!                Routine"): "the wait routine is entered at KWAIT
!                when the pseudo-op $WAIT is requested...  When
!                entered, the wait routine adjusts the return
!                address in STIC and sets up $9 for the proper
!                program status table (SPROGS)."  Lines 6-7 are
!                "adjusts the return address in STIC" (V-I,2,.32
!                then SV,2,S TIC); lines 9-10 are "sets up $9 for
!                the proper program status table" (LV,9,$L then
!                V+I,9,S PROG S, comment "-PROG STAT TABLE ADDR").
!                Line 5's comment is "-DETERMINE LEVEL OF
!                INTERRUPT" and line 8 stores that level into S M.
!                Lines 11-14 are the redundant-entry test the
!                manual describes in English: "SINTAD -- the
!                interrupted address is set to zero when entering
!                the enabled loop...  SEWAIL -- this bit in the
!                file area table is turned on when entering the
!                enabled loop.  It is turned off when $WAIT is
!                requested by mainstream with SINTAD non-zero."
!                Line 11 loads SINTAD, line 12 BXVZ's on it, line
!                14 is the "test and turn off SEWAIL" box of
!                Figure 113.  Line 15 is that figure's "IS
!                CHANNEL OPERATING".
!
!   lines 16-18  K DIG.  Figure 113 names this box KDIG and
!                reads "IS INTERRUPT STACKED ON THIS CHAN".  Line
!                16 is LVI,1,$+1. -- the one-card return that the
!                search subroutine uses -- and line 17 is
!                "B,K SERCH".  >>> C22-6728-1 printed p.21
!                ("Search and Unstack", footed "Interrupt Control
!                21") PRINTS THE WHOLE CALLING SEQUENCE IN
!                TYPESET: "LVI, 1, $+1.0 / B, KSERCH / (not found
!                return) / (found return)".  That is lines 16, 17,
!                18 and 19 of this page, in order, off a different
!                press. <<<  Line 18 is the not-found return,
!                "B,K PASS", and line 19 is the found return.
!
!   lines 19-25  the autostacked / level test.  Line 19 is
!                Figure 113's "IS REQUESTOR AUTOSTACKED -> KINTY"
!                and lines 20-25 are its "MOVE LOWER REGISTERS
!                FROM STLR TO SLRPP" / "... TO SLRMCP" pair,
!                selected on S M (the level of interrupt) by line
!                21's BB.
!
!   lines 26-29  K MISU, Figure 113's "IS UNIT IN SELECT STATUS"
!                -> "DOES UNIT AREA POINT TO RIGHT FILE AREA"
!                (lines 27-28, KV against S FI A AC then BZXE)
!                -> "IS REQUESTOR AUTOSTACKED" (line 29).
!
!   lines 30-35  the enabled loop.  Figure 113: "SET FILE AREA
!                ADDRESS IN PROGRAM STATUS" (line 30, comment
!                "-FILE A ADDR TO WAIT FIELD"), "CLEAR" SINTAD
!                (line 31), "TURN ON FILE AREA SEWAIL" (line 32),
!                then KELOOP "ACTUATED ADDRESS TO STIC" (lines
!                33-34) and out through K SUPP (line 35).
!
!   lines 36-37  K PASS, Figure 113's "CLEAR WAIT ADDRESS IN
!                PROGRAM STATUS TABLE" -> KSUPP.  Twenty-four
!                bits of zero at 0.0($9) and a branch.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-SEVEN LINES, AND NOTHING FELL OFF EITHER END -- THREE
! INDEPENDENT WAYS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SURVEY SAID 27 ROWS, 220350.40..220376.40.  It is short by
! ten and it lost the top of the page: its first location,
! 220350.40, is LINE 6.  Lines 1-5 fell off, including the
! "SLC,$" that is this page's only statement about its own origin
! and including K WAIT's own label row.  The real span is
! 220347.40..220376.40 over 37 lines.  Three ways of knowing:
!
! (1) THE CARD-SERIAL COLUMN, read at 900 dpi.  It runs
!     JA000001 on line 1 to JA000035 on line 37, unbroken, and
!     the next printed thing below line 37 is blank form.  The
!     header's own "CARD ID JA000001" names line 1's card, so the
!     top of the page is pinned by IBM twice.
!
!     >>> TWO SERIALS ARE PRINTED TWICE. <<<  JA000016 appears on
!     lines 16 AND 17, and JA000026 on lines 27 AND 28.  Verified
!     at 900 dpi on both pairs; they are not an OCR artefact and
!     not a dropped digit (JA000017 and JA000027 follow normally).
!     So 37 lines carry 35 distinct serials.  RECORDED AS A FACT
!     AND NOTHING PROPOSED -- two cards punched with the same
!     identification is a fact about the deck, and this page
!     cannot say why.
!
! (2) THE LOCATION COLUMN CLOSES ON ITSELF.  Every one of the 33
!     emitted statements' printed locations follows from the one
!     above it by exactly the width of the instruction printed
!     there -- 19 half words and 14 full words, no gap anywhere.
!     19*32 + 14*64 = 1504 bits = 23.5 words, and
!     220377.00 - 220347.40 is 24 words less 32 bits = 23.5 words.
!     A dropped row would not balance.
!
! (3) THE LINE-NUMBER COLUMN runs 1..37 with no break, and line
!     37 is followed by the form's own right-margin ruling and
!     nothing else.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHERE THE COUNTER LEAVES -- A FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION FOR p.169
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Line 37 is "B,K SUPP" at 220376.40, a HALF WORD.  >>> THE
! COUNTER LEAVES THIS PAGE AT 220377.00. <<<
!
! Printed p.169 (PDF p.176) opens with an asterisk banner --
! "*** CHANGE IO TABLE OF EXITS ***", the routine C22-6728-1
! printed p.205 says "is entered at CCHEX".  So, as predictions
! that can be killed:
!
!   * p.169's column-heading "LOCATION" field is BLANK (line 1 is
!     a banner).
!   * p.169's first LOCATED row prints 220377.00, and if it
!     prints "SLC,$" that location IS this page's exit, read off
!     IBM's own assembler.
!   * backwards: printed p.167 must leave its counter at
!     220347.40, which is what THIS page's line 4 prints.
!
! If p.169 begins anywhere else, this deck is wrong and the place
! to look is the 14 full words -- a full word misread as a half
! word loses exactly 32 bits.
!
! >>> THE FORWARD HALF OF THAT PREDICTION WAS THEN CHECKED, AND IT
!     HOLDS. <<<  PDF p.176 was rendered at 900 dpi for its first
!     six rows -- ITS LOCATION COLUMN ONLY, not transcribed, that
!     page belongs to another transcriber.  It prints:
!
!       1*  2*  3*            (asterisk banner, "*** C...")
!       4*  220377.00  *      SLC,$
!       5*  220377.00   215246.00 80  000021.36 E0   C CHEX
!       6*  220400.00   220706.01 B0
!
!     So p.169's "SLC,$" prints 220377.00, which is IBM's own
!     assembler stating the counter arriving from THIS page, and
!     it is the value above.  Its heading-row LOCATION field is
!     BLANK, as predicted (line 1 is a banner).  Its label is
!     C CHEX, which is the "entered at CCHEX" of C22-6728-1
!     printed p.205.  The join 220347.40 -> 220377.00 -> p.169 is
!     closed at both ends of this page except for the backward
!     one, which only printed p.167 can close.
!
! >>> AND THE CARD DECK ENDS WITH THE PAGE. <<<  p.169's header
!     reads "CARD ID JB000001" -- a NEW prefix.  So the JA deck is
!     exactly the 35 serials JA000001-JA000035 printed on this one
!     page, and p.169 opens JB.  That is a second, independent
!     reason nothing fell off the bottom of printed p.168: the
!     deck it belongs to does not continue.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPH CALLS, AND WHAT SETTLED EACH
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIS PAGE DROPS INK ON ROUND GLYPHS CONSTANTLY.  The LOCATION
! column alone prints "22035C.40" for 220350.40, "220351.CC" for
! 220351.00, "22037C.40", "220375.CC" and so on -- a C cannot
! occur in an octal field, so every one is a 0 with certainty and
! the page is demonstrably a heavy dropper.  Per the workflow
! that licenses nothing by itself; each call below is settled on
! what PRODUCED the glyph.
!
! (1) LINES 31 AND 36'S SECOND BYTES print "FC" and are "F0",
!     and their address fields print ".CC" for ".00".
!     Line 36's field prints "030000.CC" -- FOUR impossible C's
!     in one field -- and line 31's "031000.CC" two more.  The
!     byte is settled by the statement, not by the row: the low
!     nibble of a VFL full word is i2, the OFFSET INDEX, and
!     neither "CM0000(BU,25),SINTAD($13)" nor
!     "CM0000(BU,24),0.0($9)" has a second operand, so i2 is 0.
!     >>> AND THE PAGE PRINTS THE CONTROL: line 32, one line
!     below line 31, is "CM1111,SEWAIL($13)" and prints
!     "001000.36 F0" with a CLOSED 0 at 1800 dpi, two characters
!     from line 31's open one.  Dropout opens a closed glyph and
!     never closes an open one. <<<  printed p.18 prints that
!     same connective word six times (docs/transcription_
!     workflow.md), and printed p.158 line 15 called an
!     identical "FC" the same way.
!
! (2) LINE 8'S SECOND BYTE IS "D0", not "C0" and not "00".
!     Rendered at 1800 dpi: the first glyph has a straight
!     vertical LEFT STEM and squared corners, which is the C/D
!     discriminator the workflow names; the second is round with
!     no stem.  Corroborated by what produced it -- an ST and not
!     an L.  Line 5 on this same page is the matching LOAD, with
!     the SAME operand word "001067.20", and prints "50".  Two
!     landed decks print this exact byte for this exact
!     operation: p.75 line 12 and p.97 line 1 both carry
!     "ST(BU,3),T CAT+.47,46 -> 004771.57 80 003027.20 D0".
!
! (3) "K DIG" -- THE D.  Settled by the TYPESET MANUAL, which is
!     a different press and genuinely independent of this drum:
!     C22-6728 printed p.196, Figure 113 ("The RIO and Wait
!     Routines"), labels the box "KDIG".  The ink agrees (left
!     stem at 900 dpi) and so does the page's own second printing
!     on line 26, but neither of those is independent.
!
! (4) "SEWAIL" -- THE FINAL L.  Settled OUTRIGHT, twice over, by
!     the typeset manual: C22-6728-1 printed p.271 (Appendix A,
!     "Symbolic I-O Control Tables") tabulates
!     "1.57  SEWAIL  This indicator enables the $WAIT routine to
!     not wait again on a file which has been reactuated in a
!     fixup", and printed p.205 explains it in prose.  The
!     ADDRESS matches to the bit -- lines 14 and 32 both print
!     "000001.71" and 0o71 = 57 -- so the manual pins the name
!     AND the offset.  "SEWAIT" returns zero hits in either
!     revision.
!
! (5) "8B" vs "88" ON LINES 5 AND 26.  Not settled by ink at all.
!     The low nibble of a VFL first half word is the INDEX
!     REGISTER, and this page prints nine of them against nine
!     different "($n)" operands:
!       line  5  ($11) -> 8B      line 15  ($10) -> 8A
!       line 13  ($9)  -> 89      line 26  ($11) -> 8B
!       line 14  ($13) -> 8D      line 19  ($9)  -> 89
!       line 29  ($9)  -> 89      line 31  ($13) -> 8D
!       line 36  ($9)  -> 89      line 32  ($13) -> 8D
!     Ten rows, one page, five distinct nibbles, every one
!     agreeing with its own statement.  "88" would need an index
!     8 that no row writes.
!
! (6) "BZXE" ON LINE 28, byte "C0".  The Z is VISIBLE here and is
!     NOT flagged as ink-only: the workflow's DR/DRZ blindness is
!     about RESERVATIONS.  printed p.122 line 37 prints the same
!     instruction and the same byte ("215742.32 C0"), and that
!     deck measured the alternatives: BXE prints C2, BZXL 40,
!     BZXN is not an instruction.  Likewise lines 13 and 14 print
!     "BB" and "BBZ" ONE LINE APART, with bytes 02 and 06 -- the
!     clear bit is 0x04 and an ordinary !CHECK catches it.
!
! (7) THE SPACED AND UNSPACED SPELLINGS ARE BOTH PRINTED, on the
!     same page, for the same symbol.  A STRAP name ignores
!     blanks, so these are one symbol each; recorded because they
!     are printed:
!       "S TIC" (line 7)      vs "STIC" (line 34)
!       "SAS" (line 13)       vs "S AS" (lines 19, 29)
!       "KPASS" (line 14)     vs "K PASS" (lines 18, 36)
!       "K MISU" (line 15)    vs "KMISU" (line 26)
!       "KDIG" (line 26)      vs "K DIG" (lines 16, 28)
!       "K E LOOP" (line 29)  vs "KELOOP" (line 33)
!       "SSEL" (line 26)      vs -- (S SEL elsewhere in the
!                                 corpus)
!       "SINTAD" (lines 11,31), "SEWAIL" (lines 14,32) always
!       unspaced.
!     The statements below use the unspaced form, as every landed
!     deck does; the listing block records the printed spacing.
!
! (8) WHERE THIS DECK BEATS THE VOLUME OCR.  docs/ocr/
!     MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt line 8661 reads line 5's index as
!     "($10)".  It is "($11)", pinned by the printed byte 8B
!     against line 15's 8A for a real ($10).  The volume OCR is
!     the same ink read by a worse reader and is not a witness;
!     it was used here only as an index and to confirm no line
!     was dropped.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK -- THREE OF THEM, AND TWO ARE NOT
! ON AN "SLC" ROW
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXACTLY THREE second asterisks on this page:
!
!   line  4   220347.40 *   SLC,$
!   line 21   220362.40 *   BB,S M,$+2.32
!   line 36   220375.40 *   K PASS  CM0000(BU,24),0.0($9)
!
! Corroborated mechanically: strap/mcp_columns.py measures the
! thin column these live in as carrying 0.7 units of ink over the
! whole page, against printed p.158's 0.2 for its single glyph.
!
! RECORDED AND NOTHING PROPOSED.  docs/transcription_workflow.md
! says the asterisk has eight things it is not and that printed
! p.147's "every printed SLC,$ row carries it" was refuted by
! p.151 the same afternoon.  What this page ADDS to the record is
! the converse datum: TWO of its three asterisks are on ordinary
! instruction rows -- a BB and a VFL connective -- with no SLC,
! no label on line 21, and a label on line 36.  Whatever the mark
! means, it is not "SLC row" and it is not "label row".  No
! hypothesis is offered; see WHAT COULD NOT BE SETTLED (A).
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THIS PAGE PINS, AND WHAT IT ONLY CORROBORATES
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! PINNED TO THE BIT (the printed word changes if the offset
! changes by one):
!   S OWNER  000000.40   line  5
!   S M      215001.05   lines 8 and 21 (SCOMRG+0.05)
!   S AS     000000.31   lines 13, 19, 29
!   SEWAIL   000001.71   lines 14 and 32, TWICE
!   S CH OP  000000.32   line 15
!   S SEL    000000.47   line 26
!   SINTAD   000001.40   lines 11 and 31
!
! HALF WORD ONLY -- the .00 or .40 is a reading of bit 32 and the
! five bits below it are UNOBSERVABLE (workflow, WHAT THE ORACLE
! CANNOT SEE item 1):
!   S TIC    215265.00   lines 7 and 34 (the printed .05 is
!                        2J+1 for J=2, not S TIC's offset)
!   S PROG S 215021.00   line 10 (.23 is 2J+1 for J=9)
!   K SERCH  222020.00   line 17
!   K PASS   220375.40   lines 14 and 18  <-- this page's own
!   K INT TY 216010.40   lines 19, 23, 25
!   S TLR    215230.00   lines 22 and 24
!   S LR PP  215266.00   line 22
!   S LR MCP 215362.00   line 24
!   K SUPP   216242.00   lines 35 and 37
!   S FI A AC 000003.00  line 27 -- the printed .32 is 2J for
!                        J=13 and NOT the symbol's offset, the
!                        same finding printed p.158 made
!   $L       000010.00   line 9 (.22 is 2J for J=9); $L is the
!                        left half of the accumulator, word 8,
!                        from C22-6531 Appendix A
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT COULD NOT BE SETTLED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (A) >>> WHAT THE THREE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISKS MEAN. <<<
!     This is the page's most interesting unresolved thing,
!     because two of the three are on rows that no previous
!     hypothesis covers.  Lines 4, 21 and 36 have almost nothing
!     in common: line 4 emits no bits, line 21 is a conditional
!     branch with no label, line 36 is a labelled VFL connective.
!     Nothing was proposed, per the workflow's instruction.  WHAT
!     WOULD SETTLE IT: a census of the mark across all landed
!     decks' listing blocks, which no tool in the repo does -- the
!     comment blocks are not parsed by anything (workflow 3b).
!     That census is a repo-wide job and is explicitly NOT done
!     here.
!
! (B) "K MISU" -- WHAT IT MEANS, and therefore whether the M is
!     right for a reason rather than by ink.  The name is printed
!     twice ("K MISU" line 15, "KMISU" line 26) and both read M
!     at 900 dpi, but both share one drum.  The typeset manuals
!     do NOT contain the string: grep of both revisions of
!     C22-6728 returns zero hits for MISU, although the SAME
!     figure names KDIG, KPASS, KELOOP, KSERCH, KSUPP and KINTY.
!     So of the six routine names on this page, five have a
!     typeset witness and this one does not.  The reading
!     rejected was "K NISU"; it cannot be eliminated by the
!     assembler (a label spelling compiles to nothing) nor by any
!     !CHECK.  WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: a page that branches to
!     220366.40 from outside this page.  None is known.  IF ONE
!     TURNS UP AND SPELLS IT DIFFERENTLY, THIS DECK IS THE ONE TO
!     CHANGE.
!
! (C) TWO CARDS CARRY DUPLICATE SERIALS (JA000016, JA000026) and
!     this page cannot say why.  Both duplicates sit immediately
!     above a dashed rule, as it happens, but two instances are
!     not a pattern and the rules are printed by the form.
!
! (D) WHY THIS IS A NEW CARD DECK.  The serials restart at
!     JA000001 while printed p.167's run under another prefix.
!     printed p.151 opened IA000001 and printed p.158 ID000001
!     the same way, and printed p.158's transcriber filed the
!     prefix letters as undecoded.  Nothing in docs/ decodes
!     them and this page does not either.  What this page DOES
!     add is that a deck can be exactly ONE PAGE long: JA runs
!     JA000001-JA000035 here and p.169's header opens JB000001.
!     Whether the prefix tracks a ROUTINE (the WAIT routine is
!     exactly this page) or something else is not established.
!
! (E) THE ASTERISK BANNERS (lines 1-3) ARE NOT TRANSCRIBED
!     CHARACTER BY CHARACTER.  They are comment cards, they emit
!     no bits, and counting ~65 asterisks off a worn drum is a
!     reading nobody can check.  Line 2's text is "WAIT",
!     centred; the serials JA000001-JA000003 are counted.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ASSEMBLER LIMITATIONS HIT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! NONE.  Every statement below is the PRINTED form, handed to
! strap_asm first, and every one was accepted first time -- the
! trailing-period "LVI,1,$+1." of line 16 included.  There is
! exactly ONE substitution on the page and it is the usual one:
!
! >>> SUBSTITUTION, LABELLED LOUDLY: the page prints "SLC,$" on
!     line 4.  This deck writes the hard origin so it assembles
!     standalone.  THE PRINTED FORM IS "SLC,$" AND THE PRINTED
!     LOCATION IS 220347.40 -- that is the counter arriving from
!     printed p.167, printed by IBM's own assembler, and it is
!     the thing to compare BY HAND against p.167's exit.  A hard
!     SLC cannot disagree with an arriving counter; it overrides
!     it silently (workflow, item 2).  Note the ".40": the offset
!     inside an (8) literal is OCTAL and is written exactly as
!     printed.  Writing "(8)220347.0" by habit would move all 33
!     rows back 32 bits. <<<
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! SYMBOLS DEFINED OFF THIS PAGE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! S COMRG  215001.00  a STAND-IN, not a copy.  printed p.103 line
!                     8 DEFINES it as "SCOMRG XW,(8)520000." --
!                     an XW is a PLACE, and copying it here would
!                     land a word at this page's own counter and
!                     refuse the body merge (that is exactly what
!                     printed p.157 did and had to undo).  Only
!                     S M needs it.
SCOMRG  SYN, (8)215001.0
! S M      215001.05  printed p.103 line 15, "S M SYN(BU,1),
!                     SCOMRG+0.05  -LEVEL OF INTERRUPT".  Decks
!                     p.103 and p.128 carry it.  The dds (BU,1)
!                     is CARRIED, not omitted: line 21's BB and
!                     line 8's ST both take their length from the
!                     symbol.  LINES 8 AND 21 PIN IT TO THE BIT.
SM      SYN(BU,1), SCOMRG+0.05
! S OWNER  000000.40  printed p.99, "-PRIORITY LEVEL", 0.32
!                     decimal = 0o40.  SIX landed decks carry it
!                     (counted), p.123 and p.128 among them.
!                     LINE 5 PINS IT TO THE BIT.
SOWNER  SYN(BU,1), 0.32
! S CH OP  000000.32  printed p.99 line 22 "-CHAN OPERATING",
!                     0.26 decimal = 0o32.  TEN landed decks
!                     carry it (counted, not estimated).  LINE 15 PINS IT TO THE BIT.
SCHOP   SYN(BU,1), 0.26
! S SEL    000000.47  printed p.99 line 32 "-SELECT", 0.39
!                     decimal = 0o47.  NINE landed decks carry
!                     it (counted, not estimated).  LINE 26 PINS IT TO THE BIT.
SSEL    SYN(BU,1), 0.39
! S AS     000000.31  printed p.109 line 8 "-AUTO STACK STATUS",
!                     .25 decimal = 0o31.  Six landed decks carry
!                     it.  LINES 13, 19 AND 29 PIN IT TO THE BIT.
SAS     SYN(BU,1), .25
! SINTAD   000001.40  printed p.102 line 8 "-INTERRUPTED
!                     ADDRESS", 1.32 decimal = 0o40, nineteen
!                     bits.  C22-6728-1 printed p.271 tabulates
!                     "1.32 - 1.50 SINTAD".  LINES 11 AND 31 PIN
!                     IT TO THE BIT; line 31's (BU,25) overrides
!                     the length, line 11's LV does not.
SINTAD  SYN(BU,19), 1.32
! SEWAIL   000001.71  >>> printed p.102 line 9 DEFINES IT:
!                     "SEWAIL SYN(BU,1),1.57  -LOOP BIT FOR
!                     $WAIT", and decks/mcp_bootstrap_p102.strap
!                     line 272 already carries exactly this card
!                     with exactly this dds. <<<  So this deck
!                     AGREES with the defining page and the body
!                     merge has nothing to refuse.  The manual
!                     agrees independently (item 4 above).  LINES
!                     14 AND 32 PIN IT TO THE BIT, twice.
SEWAIL  SYN(BU,1), 1.57
! S FI A AC 000003.00 printed p.101 line 13 "-FILE AREA
!                     ACTIVATED".  EIGHT landed decks carry 3.0
!                     with (BU,18) (counted, not estimated).  LINE 27 CHECKS THE HALF WORD
!                     ONLY -- its printed .32 is 2J for J=13.
SFIAAC  SYN(BU,18), 3.0
! S TIC    215265.00  the STored Instruction Counter; C22-6728
!                     prose, "the saved IC is stored in STIC",
!                     and NINETEEN landed decks carry this SYN at
!                     this address, with a twentieth (printed
!                     p.116) defining it by VF (counted).  LINES 7 AND 34 CHECK THE
!                     HALF WORD.
STIC    SYN, (8)215265.0
! S PROG S 215021.00  the program status table; printed p.109
!                     line 7 DEFINES it ("SPROGS DRZ(BU,64),2").
!                     SEVEN landed decks carry it (counted),
!                     p.117, p.118, p.123 and p.128 among them.
!                     LINE 10 CHECKS THE HALF WORD.
SPROGS  SYN, (8)215021.0
! S TLR    215230.00  printed p.116 line 13 DEFINES it
!                     ("STLR DRZ(BU,64),29").  LINES 22 AND 24
!                     CHECK THE HALF WORD.
STLR    SYN, (8)215230.0
! S LR PP  215266.00  printed p.116 line 18 DEFINES it.  LINE 22
!                     CHECKS THE HALF WORD -- and prints the
!                     identical word printed p.123 line 43
!                     prints, "215230.00 80 215266.36 20".
SLRPP   SYN, (8)215266.0
! S LR MCP 215362.00  printed p.116 line 26 DEFINES it.  LINE 24
!                     CHECKS THE HALF WORD.
SLRMCP  SYN, (8)215362.0
! K SERCH  222020.00  the search subroutine; C22-6728-1 prints
!                     its calling sequence as "B, KSERCH".  Deck
!                     p.142 carries this SYN.  LINE 17 CHECKS THE
!                     HALF WORD.
KSERCH  SYN, (8)222020.0
! K INT TY 216010.40  printed p.123 line 47 is its label row
!                     ("KINTTY LV,1,STOELO($13)").  Decks p.140,
!                     p.141 and p.142 carry this SYN.  LINES 19,
!                     23 AND 25 CHECK THE HALF WORD, and the .40
!                     is REAL -- bit 32 is visible.
KINTTY  SYN, (8)216010.40
! K SUPP   216242.00  the service-op return routine; defined
!                     printed p.128.  NINETEEN landed decks SYN
!                     it at this address and ELEVEN carry the
!                     printed word "216242.10" in a check or a
!                     listing row (counted).  LINES 35 AND 37 CHECK THE
!                     HALF WORD; the BIT offset stays unreadable.
KSUPP   SYN, (8)216242.0
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE PAGE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! lines 1-3 are the asterisk banner "********** WAIT **********",
! three comment cards (JA000001-JA000003) emitting nothing; see
! WHAT COULD NOT BE SETTLED (E).
!
        SLC, (8)220347.40            ! line  4  printed "SLC,$" at 220347.40 *
KWAIT   L(BU,1),SOWNER($11),-18      ! line  5  -DETERMINE LEVEL OF INTERRUPT
        V-I,2,.32                    ! line  6
        SV,2,STIC                    ! line  7  printed "SV,2,S TIC"
        ST(BU,1),SM,-18              ! line  8  printed "ST(BU,1),S M,-18"
        LV,9,$L                      ! line  9
        V+I,9,SPROGS                 ! line 10  -PROG STAT TABLE ADDR
        LV,1,SINTAD($13)             ! line 11
        BXVZ,$+2.32                  ! line 12
        BB,SAS($9),$+2.0             ! line 13  printed "BB,SAS($9),$+2.0"
        BBZ,SEWAIL($13),KPASS        ! line 14
        BB,SCHOP($10),KMISU          ! line 15  printed "BB,S CH OP($10),K MISU"
KDIG    LVI,1,$+1.                   ! line 16  printed "K DIG"
        B,KSERCH                     ! line 17  printed "B,K SERCH"
        B,KPASS                      ! line 18  printed "B,K PASS"
        BB,SAS($9),KINTTY            ! line 19  printed "BB,S AS($9),K INT TY"
        LCI,15,30                    ! line 20
        BB,SM,$+2.32                 ! line 21  printed "BB,S M,$+2.32" *
        T,15,STLR,SLRPP              ! line 22  printed "T,15,S TLR,S LR PP"
        B,KINTTY                     ! line 23  printed "B,K INT TY"
        T,15,STLR,SLRMCP             ! line 24  printed "T,15,S TLR,S LR MCP"
        B,KINTTY                     ! line 25  printed "B,K INT TY"
KMISU   BB,SSEL($11),KDIG            ! line 26  printed "KMISU"
        KV,13,SFIAAC($12)            ! line 27  printed "KV,13,S FI AAC($12)"
        BZXE,KDIG                    ! line 28  printed "BZXE,K DIG"
        BB,SAS($9),KELOOP            ! line 29  printed "BB,S AS($9),K E LOOP"
        SV,13,0.0($9)                ! line 30  -FILE A ADDR TO WAIT FIELD
        CM0000(BU,25),SINTAD($13)    ! line 31
        CM1111,SEWAIL($13)           ! line 32  no dds; length comes from SEWAIL
KELOOP  V-I,2,1.32                   ! line 33  printed "KELOOP"
        SV,2,STIC                    ! line 34  printed "SV,2,STIC", unspaced
        B,KSUPP                      ! line 35  printed "B,K SUPP"
KPASS   CM0000(BU,24),0.0($9)        ! line 36  printed "K PASS" *
        B,KSUPP                      ! line 37  printed "B,K SUPP"
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE PRINTED LISTING, AS THE ORACLE READS IT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Five symbols and thirty-three rows, covering every one of the
! 47 emitted half words.  Each row is the printed BINARY OUTPUT
! column verbatim, with the three glyph calls recorded in their
! notes.  LINE | LOCATION | BINARY OUTPUT | NAME | STATEMENT |
! comment | card serial:
!
!   line  1                                                       **********  WAIT  **********             JA000001
!   line  2                                                       (banner)                                 JA000002
!   line  3                                                       (banner)                                 JA000003
!   line  4*  220347.40  *                                        SLC,$                                    JA000004
!   line  5*  220347.40     000000.40 8B  001067.20 50   K WAIT   L(BU,1),S OWNER($11),-18  -DETERMINE LEVEL OF INTERRUPT   JA000005
!   line  6*  220350.40     000000.45 0D                          V-I,2,.32                                JA000006
!   line  7*  220351.00     215265.05 30                          SV,2,S TIC                               JA000007
!   line  8*  220351.40     215001.05 80  001067.20 D0            ST(BU,1),S M,-18                         JA000008
!   line  9*  220352.40     000010.22 30                          LV,9,$L                                  JA000009
!   line 10*  220353.00     215021.23 05                          V+I,9,S PROG S           -PROG STAT TABLE ADDR           JA000010
!   line 11*  220353.40     000001.42 3D                          LV,1,SINTAD($13)                         JA000011
!   line 12*  220354.00     220356.71 42                          BXVZ,$+2.32                              JA000012
!   line 13*  220354.40     000000.31 89  220356.74 02            BB,SAS($9),$+2.0                         JA000013
!   line 14*  220355.40     000001.71 8D  220375.74 06            BBZ,SEWAIL($13),KPASS                    JA000014
!   line 15*  220356.40     000000.32 8A  220366.74 02            BB,S CH OP($10),K MISU                   JA000015
!   line 16*  220357.40     220360.43 01                 K DIG    LVI,1,$+1.                               JA000016
!   line 17*  220360.00     222020.10 00                          B,K SERCH                                JA000016  (sic, duplicate)
!   line 18*  220360.40     220375.50 00                          B,K PASS                                 JA000017
!   line 19*  220361.00     000000.31 89  216010.74 02            BB,S AS($9),K INT TY                     JA000018
!   line 20*  220362.00     000036.37 02                          LCI,15,30                                JA000019
!   line 21*  220362.40  *   215001.05 80  220365.34 02           BB,S M,$+2.32                            JA000020
!   line 22*  220363.40     215230.00 80  215266.36 20            T,15,S TLR,S LR PP                       JA000021
!   line 23*  220364.40     216010.50 00                          B,K INT TY                               JA000022
!   line 24*  220365.00     215230.00 80  215362.36 20            T,15,S TLR,S LR MCP                      JA000023
!   line 25*  220366.00     216010.50 00                          B,K INT TY                               JA000024
!   line 26*  220366.40     000000.47 8B  220357.74 02   KMISU    BB,SSEL($11),KDIG                        JA000025
!   line 27*  220367.40     000003.32 9C                          KV,13,S FI AAC($12)                      JA000026
!   line 28*  220370.00     220357.72 C0                          BZXE,K DIG                               JA000026  (sic, duplicate)
!   line 29*  220370.40     000000.31 89  220374.34 02            BB,S AS($9),K E LOOP                     JA000027
!   line 30*  220371.40     000000.33 39                          SV,13,0.0($9)            -FILE A ADDR TO WAIT FIELD      JA000028
!   line 31*  220372.00     000001.40 8D  031000.00 F0            CM0000(BU,25),SINTAD($13)                JA000029
!   line 32*  220373.00     000001.71 8D  001000.36 F0            CM1111,SEWAIL($13)                       JA000030
!   line 33*  220374.00     000001.45 0D                 KELOOP   V-I,2,1.32                               JA000031
!   line 34*  220374.40     215265.05 30                          SV,2,STIC                                JA000032
!   line 35*  220375.00     216242.10 00                          B,K SUPP                                 JA000033
!   line 36*  220375.40  *   000000.00 89  030000.00 F0  K PASS   CM0000(BU,24),0.0($9)                    JA000034
!   line 37*  220376.40     216242.10 00                          B,K SUPP                                 JA000035
!
! Lines 31 and 36 are recorded above with the bytes and fields AS
! ESTABLISHED (F0, .00); the drum prints them "FC" and ".CC" and
! GLYPH CALL (1) says why that is a 0.  Line 8's D0 is recorded
! as read at 1800 dpi, GLYPH CALL (2).
!
!SYM KWAIT  220347.40
!SYM KDIG   220357.40
!SYM KMISU  220366.40
!SYM KELOOP 220374.00
!SYM KPASS  220375.40
!
!CHECK 220347.40 | 000000.40 8B 001067.20 50 | line 5 K WAIT L(BU,1),S OWNER($11),-18 -DETERMINE LEVEL OF INTERRUPT -- .40 is S OWNER at 0.32 decimal, pinned to the bit; 8B's low nibble B is index $11 (the volume OCR reads $10 and is wrong); the second word's 001067.20 carries length 1 and offset -18 (110 = 0o156 in the 7-bit field), and 50 is the LOAD against line 8's STORE D0
!CHECK 220350.40 | 000000.45 0D | line 6 V-I,2,.32 -- .32 DECIMAL is 0o40, plus the index-arithmetic 2J+1 = 5 for J=2; had .32 been read octal this row would fail. The printed SLC,$ on line 4 gives this page's arriving counter as 220347.40
!CHECK 220351.00 | 215265.05 30 | line 7 SV,2,S TIC -- .05 is 2J+1 for J=2 and NOT any part of S TIC's offset, so this row checks the HALF WORD only
!CHECK 220351.40 | 215001.05 80 001067.20 D0 | line 8 ST(BU,1),S M,-18 -- .05 is S M at SCOMRG+0.05, pinned to the bit; no index so the byte is 80; the second byte prints D0 at 1800 dpi (straight left stem, squared corners) and is the STORE counterpart of line 5's LOAD 50 against the IDENTICAL operand word 001067.20 -- decks p.75 and p.97 print this same byte for ST
!CHECK 220352.40 | 000010.22 30 | line 9 LV,9,$L -- 0o10 is word 8, the left half of the accumulator (C22-6531 Appendix A); .22 is 2J = 18 for J=9
!CHECK 220353.00 | 215021.23 05 | line 10 V+I,9,S PROG S -PROG STAT TABLE ADDR -- .23 is 2J+1 = 19 for J=9, so S PROG S is checked to the HALF WORD only
!CHECK 220353.40 | 000001.42 3D | line 11 LV,1,SINTAD($13) -- .42 is SINTAD's 1.32 decimal (0o40) plus 2J = 2 for J=1, which pins the symbol to the bit; 3D's low nibble D is index $13
!CHECK 220354.00 | 220356.71 42 | line 12 BXVZ,$+2.32 -- $ is 220354.00 so the target is 220356.40 (.32 DECIMAL = 0o40) and 0o31 is the XVZ indicator, 0o40 OR 0o31 = 0o71. Line 28's BZXE uses 0o32 the same way, which is what makes the two readings mutually checking
!CHECK 220354.40 | 000000.31 89 220356.74 02 | line 13 BB,SAS($9),$+2.0 -- .31 is S AS at .25 decimal, pinned to the bit; 89 is index $9; the target 220356.40 prints as .74 (0o40 OR 0o34) and the byte 02 is BB against line 14's BBZ 06, ONE LINE APART, which is what settles both
!CHECK 220355.40 | 000001.71 8D 220375.74 06 | line 14 BBZ,SEWAIL($13),KPASS -- .71 is SEWAIL at 1.57 decimal, the exact offset C22-6728-1 printed p.271 tabulates and printed p.102 line 9 defines; 8D is index $13; byte 06 is BBZ (BB prints 02, line 13) and the Z is VISIBLE, not ink-only
!CHECK 220356.40 | 000000.32 8A 220366.74 02 | line 15 BB,S CH OP($10),K MISU -- .32 is S CH OP at 0.26 decimal, pinned to the bit; 8A is index $10, which is what pins line 5's 8B to $11 by contrast; the target is this page's own K MISU at 220366.40
!CHECK 220357.40 | 220360.43 01 | line 16 K DIG LVI,1,$+1. -- the trailing period is the printed form and strap_asm takes it; $+1. is 220360.40 and 0o40 OR 0o03 is LVI's .03 (the operation bits the workflow names); byte 01 is J=1
!CHECK 220360.00 | 222020.10 00 | line 17 B,K SERCH -- a plain B prints target+.10, so this checks K SERCH's HALF WORD at 222020.00 and nothing below bit 32
!CHECK 220360.40 | 220375.50 00 | line 18 B,K PASS -- K PASS is this page's own line 36 at 220375.40; 0o40 OR 0o10 = 0o50, so bit 32 IS checked here and the label row confirms it
!CHECK 220361.00 | 000000.31 89 216010.74 02 | line 19 BB,S AS($9),K INT TY -- the same .31 and 89 as line 13 with a different target; K INT TY's .40 prints as .74 and is REAL, bit 32 being visible
!CHECK 220362.00 | 000036.37 02 | line 20 LCI,15,30 -- the datum 30 DECIMAL prints as 0o36 in the address field, and .37 is 2J+1 = 31 for J=15
!CHECK 220362.40 | 215001.05 80 220365.34 02 | line 21 BB,S M,$+2.32 -- the SAME first half word as line 8, which is two readings of S M's 215001.05 off two different operations; the target is $+2.32 = 220365.00 (.32 DECIMAL), printing .34; this row carries the second of the page's three location-column asterisks
!CHECK 220363.40 | 215230.00 80 215266.36 20 | line 22 T,15,S TLR,S LR PP -- bit-identical to printed p.123 line 43's own row, a landed deck printing the same word for the same statement; both symbols checked to the HALF WORD
!CHECK 220364.40 | 216010.50 00 | line 23 B,K INT TY -- target+.10 on 216010.40
!CHECK 220365.00 | 215230.00 80 215362.36 20 | line 24 T,15,S TLR,S LR MCP -- the same transmit against S LR MCP; the two T rows differ ONLY in the second address, which is what makes them each other's control
!CHECK 220366.00 | 216010.50 00 | line 25 B,K INT TY -- the same half word as line 23, printed twice on one page
!CHECK 220366.40 | 000000.47 8B 220357.74 02 | line 26 KMISU BB,SSEL($11),KDIG -- .47 is S SEL at 0.39 decimal, pinned to the bit; 8B is index $11 again; the target is this page's own K DIG at 220357.40
!CHECK 220367.40 | 000003.32 9C | line 27 KV,13,S FI AAC($12) -- .32 is 2J = 26 for J=13 and NOT S FI A AC's offset, so the symbol is half-word-pinned only; 9C's low nibble C is index $12, and printed p.158 line 10 prints this identical word
!CHECK 220370.00 | 220357.72 C0 | line 28 BZXE,K DIG -- 0o40 OR 0o32 = 0o72 on K DIG's 220357.40, the same indicator-bit construction line 12 uses with 0o31; byte C0 is BZXE and printed p.122 line 37 prints the identical byte for the identical instruction, so the C is a real C and not this page's damaged 0
!CHECK 220370.40 | 000000.31 89 220374.34 02 | line 29 BB,S AS($9),K E LOOP -- the third printing of S AS's .31 on this page; the target is this page's own KELOOP at 220374.00, printing .34
!CHECK 220371.40 | 000000.33 39 | line 30 SV,13,0.0($9) -FILE A ADDR TO WAIT FIELD -- .33 is 2J+1 = 27 for J=13; 39's low nibble is index $9; the address is a literal 0.0, not a symbol
!CHECK 220372.00 | 000001.40 8D 031000.00 F0 | line 31 CM0000(BU,25),SINTAD($13) -- .40 is SINTAD at 1.32 decimal, pinned to the bit; 0o031 in the second word is the length 25; the page prints that field ".CC" and the byte "FC", both demonstrably 0 -- a C cannot occur in an octal field, CM0000 has no second operand so i2 is 0, and line 32 one row below prints a CLOSED 0 in the same byte position
!CHECK 220373.00 | 000001.71 8D 001000.36 F0 | line 32 CM1111,SEWAIL($13) -- NO dds on the card, so the length comes from SEWAIL's own (BU,1); the resulting connective word 001000.36 F0 is the one printed p.18 prints six times, and its closed 0 is the control for line 31's open one. .71 is SEWAIL at 1.57, its second bit-exact printing on this page
!CHECK 220374.00 | 000001.45 0D | line 33 KELOOP V-I,2,1.32 -- 1.32 DECIMAL is word 1 bit 0o40, plus 2J+1 = 5; identical to line 6 except for the word, which is what shows the .32 is read the same way in both
!CHECK 220374.40 | 215265.05 30 | line 34 SV,2,STIC -- bit-identical to line 7, and the card spells the name UNSPACED where line 7 spaces it; a STRAP name ignores blanks and the printed word proves it
!CHECK 220375.00 | 216242.10 00 | line 35 B,K SUPP -- target+.10 on K SUPP's 216242.00; eleven landed decks already carry this printed word, so this row corroborates rather than establishes, and K SUPP's BIT offset stays unreadable
!CHECK 220375.40 | 000000.00 89 030000.00 F0 | line 36 K PASS CM0000(BU,24),0.0($9) -- 0o030 is the length 24; the page prints "030000.CC FC" with four impossible C's and the byte is F0 for the same reason as line 31; 89 is index $9; this row carries the third location-column asterisk
!CHECK 220376.40 | 216242.10 00 | line 37 B,K SUPP -- the same half word as line 35, printed twice. A HALF WORD, so THE COUNTER LEAVES THIS PAGE AT 220377.00, which is what printed p.169's first located row must print
